  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22832v1</id>
    <title>Efficient Fine-Grained GPU Performance Modeling for Distributed Deep Learning of LLM</title>
    <updated>2025-09-26T18:38:25Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22832v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22832v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Training Large Language Models(LLMs) is one of the most compute-intensive tasks in high-performance computing. Predicting end-to-end training time for multi-billion parameter models distributed across hundreds of GPUs remains challenging due to complex interactions between transformer components, parallelism strategies(data, model, pipeline, tensor), and multi-tier communication. Learned models require costly sampling, while analytical models often struggle with real-world network and hardware complexities. We address this by decomposing LLMs into core computational primitives and modeling them with: (1) operator-level decomposition for fine-grained analysis; (2) lightweight sampling based hardware-aware prediction models for key operations; (3) an end-to-end prediction system integrating these components across complex parallelization strategies. Crucially, our methodology has been validated on two large-scale HPC systems. Our framework achieves low average prediction errors-4.98\% on Perlmutter(A100) and 9.38\% on Vista(GH200)-for models up to 20B parameters across 128 GPUs. Importantly, it runs entirely on CPUs, enabling rapid iteration over hardware configurations and training strategies without costly on-cluster experimentation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.DC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-26T18:38:25Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.DC"/>
    <author>
      <name>Biyao Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
